# Clean Architecture (Backend)

## Layer dependency rule

Dependencies point **inward only**:

```
presentation → application → domain ← infrastructure
```

`domain` depends on nothing else in this list. Everything else depends inward toward it, never sideways or outward.

## Layer responsibilities

| Layer | Contains | Must NOT contain |
|---|---|---|
| **domain** | Entities, value objects, state-machine rules, repository/gateway ports (interfaces) | Spring, JPA, Jackson, HTTP |
| **application** | Use cases (one class per user-facing operation), use-case input/output models, orchestration | JPA entities, SQL, `@RestController`, Jackson annotations |
| **infrastructure** | JPA entities, Spring Data repositories, port adapters, Redis/Kafka clients, mappers between JPA entities and domain entities | Business rules, HTTP concerns |
| **presentation** | `@RestController`s, request/response DTOs, `@RestControllerAdvice`, mappers between use-case output and HTTP DTOs | Business rules, persistence concerns |

## Package shape

Each bounded module (`event`, `endpoint`, `delivery`, `outbox`, …) carries all four layers as subpackages:

```
com.reliablewebhooks.event/
├── domain/           Event, EventState, EventRepository (port)
├── application/       IngestEventUseCase, GetEventUseCase, IdempotencyGateway (port), ...
├── infrastructure/    EventJpaEntity, SpringDataEventRepository, EventRepositoryAdapter, EventMapper
└── presentation/       EventController, IdempotentReplay, EventIngestRequest, EventResponse
```

A cross-cutting technical concern with no domain rules of its own (idempotency caching, pagination helpers, the global `ProblemDetail` advice) is infrastructure or presentation, not domain — it doesn't earn its own bounded module's full four layers.

Boilerplate (getters, constructors) in any layer is generated via Lombok, not hand-written — see `.claude/lombok.mdc` for which annotations belong on which layer. JPA-entity-to-domain-entity mapping in `infrastructure` is generated via MapStruct where both directions are in real use — see `.claude/mapstruct.mdc`.

## Examples

```java
// ❌ BAD — JPA entity leaking into a use case
public EventResponse ingest(EventIngestRequest request) {
    Event event = eventRepository.save(new Event(...)); // JPA entity
    return EventResponse.from(event); // HTTP DTO built in the use case
}

// ✅ GOOD — use case depends on the domain port and returns a domain view
public EventView ingest(IngestEventCommand command) {
    Event event = Event.receive(command.producerId(), command.idempotencyKey(), command.payload());
    return EventView.from(eventRepository.save(event));
}
```

## Bounded modules (this backend)

- `event` — ingestion, idempotency-key handling, outbox write, Event state machine (docs/adr/0002, 0003, 0005)
- `endpoint` — consumer endpoint registration, HMAC secret issuance, circuit-breaker state and the Resilience4j-backed `EndpointCircuitBreaker` port (docs/adr/0006, 0007)
- `delivery` — per (Event, Endpoint) delivery state, the Kafka `DeliveryPublisher` port (main/retry-band/DLQ routing), the `EndpointDeliveryClient`/`DeliveryDedupChecker` ports, and `delivery/application/ProcessDeliveryAttemptUseCase` — the delivery worker driven by `delivery/infrastructure/DeliveryAttemptConsumer`'s `@KafkaListener` (docs/adr/0001, 0002, 0004, 0005, 0006, 0007)
- `attempt` — one Attempt per real HTTP try (or dedup/circuit-open short-circuit) belonging to a Delivery (docs/adr/0005)
- `outbox` — transactional outbox entries and the poller that fans them out to Deliveries and publishes to Kafka, `outbox/application/PublishOutboxEntriesUseCase` (docs/adr/0003, 0004)
- `idempotency` — the Redis-backed ingest-time replay cache (docs/adr/0002); infrastructure only, no domain layer of its own
